WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:29.980
Sophie Adano and her crew 12 crewmates undocked from the International Space Station today after nearly eight months aboard. This is Fascinating Frontiers, episode 215. The European Space Agency astronaut completed the Undocking at around 1205 Greenwich Mean Time on 7 October, 2026. The Dragon spacecraft then performed a series of burns to move away from the station before beginning the return journey. The capsule heat shield protects against

00:00:29.980 --> 00:00:36.700
plasma temperatures as high as 1600 degrees Celsius during reentry. The crew is planned to splash

00:00:36.700 --> 00:00:43.700
down off the coast of California on 8 October at 1534 Greenwich Mean Time, where a recovery

00:00:43.700 --> 00:00:49.620
team will pick them up. Sophie will then fly to Cologne, Germany for post-flight activities at the

00:00:49.620 --> 00:00:56.980
European Astronaut Center and the nearby Envyhab facility. She launched on 13 February 2026,

00:00:56.980 --> 00:01:02.200
complex, completed three spacewalks totaling 19 hours and 42 minutes, and reached the

00:01:02.200 --> 00:01:07.020
mark of 200 consecutive days aboard the station on 3 September, 2026.

00:01:07.820 --> 00:01:12.180
The Epsilon mission set a new European record for the longest single spaceflight.

00:01:12.860 --> 00:01:18.520
Now shifting to chemistry around young stars, an international team using the Atacama large

00:01:18.520 --> 00:01:24.220
millimeter sub-millimeter array, studied 11 nearby sun-like infant stars through the Compass

00:01:24.220 --> 00:01:30.400
large program. The survey requires more than 100 hours of telescope time to catalog chemical

00:01:30.400 --> 00:01:36.340
compositions around the protostars. Results show young stars are surrounded by organic molecules,

00:01:36.560 --> 00:01:42.780
including methanol, that may one day enrich planets. Seven papers in astronomy and astrophysics

00:01:42.780 --> 00:01:48.900
present the first analyses of the data set. Yes, Jörgensen of the University of Copenhagen serves

00:01:48.900 --> 00:01:54.000
as principal investigator. The program maps how different chemicals are distributed around

00:01:54.000 --> 00:01:59.640
young stars on scales comparable to our own solar system. Point a radio dish at nothing

00:01:59.640 --> 00:02:04.320
for a thousand hours, and the faint molecular fingerprints of methanol emerge around

00:02:04.320 --> 00:02:09.720
these 11 proto-stars. The survey reveals how organic material is distributed on solar

00:02:09.720 --> 00:02:14.920
system scales before planets assemble. Yes, Jorgensen notes that these ingredients may

00:02:14.920 --> 00:02:20.580
influence conditions on future worlds and the origin of life. The seven new papers begin the

00:02:20.580 --> 00:02:24.940
systematic comparison of protostars at different stages and in different environments.

00:02:25.660 --> 00:02:31.100
What remains unknown is exactly how much of this methanol survives the journey into planet-forming

00:02:31.100 --> 00:02:34.420
disks and eventually into oceans or atmospheres.

00:02:35.140 --> 00:02:40.380
Picture a cloud of gas and dust the size of a city block collapsing under its own gravity,

00:02:40.840 --> 00:02:43.680
while temperatures hover just above absolute zero.

00:02:44.340 --> 00:02:50.360
Inside that frigid envelope, carbon monoxide molecules land on microscopic ice grains and

00:02:50.360 --> 00:02:56.600
combine with hydrogen atoms one by one until they form methanol, a molecule with four atoms

00:02:56.600 --> 00:02:59.940
that can later participate in far more complex chemistry.

00:03:00.740 --> 00:03:06.640
The process happens on time scales of roughly 100,000 years at densities a million times higher

00:03:06.640 --> 00:03:12.280
than the air in this room, yet the grains themselves are only a few tenths of a micrometer across.

00:03:13.140 --> 00:03:18.380
Alma now resolves these zones down to distances comparable to the orbit of Neptune around

00:03:18.380 --> 00:03:24.560
our own Sun. Revealing that methanol appears in both warm inner regions and colder outer layers.

00:03:25.160 --> 00:03:30.880
The open question is whether the methanol we detect today formed primarily on grain surfaces

00:03:30.880 --> 00:03:36.820
or received an extra boost from gas phase reactions once the protostar began to heat it's surroundings.

00:03:37.520 --> 00:03:42.060
And nobody has yet measured the precise branching ratios under those exact conditions.

00:03:42.800 --> 00:03:48.220
On a different note, India has completed integration work and testing of the crew module for the

00:03:48.220 --> 00:03:50.920
first uncrewed orbital flight of Gagagnon.

00:03:51.380 --> 00:03:53.900
The launch is expected within the next two months

00:03:53.900 --> 00:03:55.760
on board the first test vehicle.

00:03:56.460 --> 00:03:59.060
The spacecraft is now ready for its initial orbital

00:03:59.060 --> 00:03:59.820
demonstration.

00:04:00.420 --> 00:04:03.680
Lexed up, the Hara spacecraft launched two years ago

00:04:03.680 --> 00:04:06.620
and is now preparing sustained breaking maneuvers

00:04:06.620 --> 00:04:08.680
to slow down for its target asteroids.

00:04:09.380 --> 00:04:12.420
The small car-sized probe will approach the Didymos

00:04:12.420 --> 00:04:15.260
double asteroid system after its two-year journey.

00:04:15.260 --> 00:04:22.300
Hara and its CubeSats, Milani and Huventas, will explore Didemos and Demorphos, the first

00:04:22.300 --> 00:04:25.860
solar system object to have its orbit shifted by human action.

00:04:26.700 --> 00:04:32.320
The short list for the 2026 Space News Icon Awards honors 43 leaders and accomplishments

00:04:32.320 --> 00:04:33.980
across the space industry.

00:04:34.660 --> 00:04:40.960
Finalists include Mark Dankberg for Satellite Communications work at BSAT, John Eves-Legal

00:04:40.960 --> 00:04:47.580
for European launch capabilities at CNES and Arian Space, and Chris Colasey for leadership

00:04:47.580 --> 00:04:53.840
at the National Reconnaissance Office. Karen Starrgerman is recognized for shaping NASA Earth

00:04:53.840 --> 00:05:00.100
Science Enterprise. Coyote Smith is noted for contributions to space power strategy and education.

00:05:00.960 --> 00:05:06.620
Winners will be announced on 1 December in Washington, D.C. A University of South Hampton Citizen

00:05:06.620 --> 00:05:11.840
and science project on Zuniverse called Black Hole Hunters invites amateur astronomers

00:05:11.840 --> 00:05:15.580
to search for hidden examples using tens of thousands of volunteers.

00:05:16.320 --> 00:05:21.360
Stellar evolution models predict hundreds of millions of black holes in the galaxy, yet

00:05:21.360 --> 00:05:22.880
only 70 have been found.

00:05:23.560 --> 00:05:30.680
Luna 3 launched from Bikonor Cosmodrome on 4 October 1959, and reached the moon two years after

00:05:30.680 --> 00:05:31.160
Sputnik.

00:05:31.160 --> 00:05:38.060
The spacecraft captured the first images of the lunar far side after a rocket-burned error on an earlier mission.

00:05:38.720 --> 00:05:42.500
The photographs marked another Soviet milestone in lunar exploration.

00:05:43.180 --> 00:05:49.620
A Belgian scientist received recognition for the Ice Cube project that detects neutrinos in Antarctic ice.

00:05:50.280 --> 00:05:56.180
Data from the detector are available live and historically, allowing new visualization tools to be built.

00:05:56.180 --> 00:06:01.540
NASA highlights Alaska role in the growing space economy through its northern location

00:06:01.540 --> 00:06:02.480
and infrastructure.

00:06:02.900 --> 00:06:08.160
The state supports launch and tracking activities that expand commercial and government operations.

00:06:08.920 --> 00:06:14.180
Northrop Grumman's Cygnus-XL spacecraft will depart the International Space Station on

00:06:14.180 --> 00:06:19.440
9 October after delivering more than 11,000 pounds of supplies and experiments.

00:06:20.100 --> 00:06:25.020
NASA will provide live coverage of the undocking and departure beginning at 12.30.

00:06:25.020 --> 00:06:31.580
The uncrewed cargo flight of Starliner 1 has been delayed to no earlier than 14 January

00:06:31.580 --> 00:06:32.580
2027.

00:06:33.460 --> 00:06:35.800
Earlier plans had targeted a December window.

00:06:36.620 --> 00:06:41.180
Today, astronomy picture of the day features the supernova remnant PA-30.

00:06:41.740 --> 00:06:46.180
The image shows the expanding debris from a stellar explosion captured in detail.

00:06:46.940 --> 00:06:52.260
Satellite records show Arctic sea ice covering less of the ocean in both winter and summer periods.

00:06:52.260 --> 00:06:59.640
The 2026 minimum continues the observed long-term decline. NASA challenges viewers to identify

00:06:59.640 --> 00:07:05.160
the location of a new satellite image and explain why the scene is scientifically interesting.

00:07:05.880 --> 00:07:09.720
Keep an eye on the splashdown off California expected tomorrow morning.

